<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Stephen of Perm</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1340–<a href="/years/1396">1396</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Saint Stephen of Perm</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Stephen of Perm was a Russian missionary monk from Ustyug who converted many of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/komi-peoples">Komi</a> people to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> from 1379 and became the first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a> of Perm in 1383. He created the Old Permic alphabet for the Komi language and translated liturgical texts into it. He is venerated as a saint of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-orthodox-church">Russian Orthodox Church</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/stephen-of-perm.jpg" alt="Stephen of Perm" width="320" height="479" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Лицевое житие преподобного Сергия Радонежского · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bishop_Stefan_of_Perm.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Stephen was born around 1340 in Ustyug in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, where the native <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/komi-peoples">Komi</a>, also called Zyrians or western Permians, lived beside a minority of Russians. His father was a cathedral cleric, and Stephen mastered reading and writing in Russian early and became a lector. He took his monastic vows in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rostov">Rostov</a>, where he learned Greek and the trade of a copyist, and befriended Epiphanius the Wise, who later wrote his biography, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sergius-of-radonezh">Sergius of Radonezh</a>, though he did not become Sergius's disciple.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>Around 1370 Stephen began to create an alphabet for the Zyrians, without Russian influence, despite the trilingual heresy current in some Byzantine and Russian circles. He then travelled to the lands along the Vychegda and Vym rivers and began converting the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/komi-peoples">Komi</a>. Where contemporary missionaries imposed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> or Church Slavonic, he learned the people's language and traditions and devised a distinct writing system, the second oldest for a Uralic language. His destruction of pagan idols, such as holy birches, earned him the anger of some Permians. Pimen, head of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-orthodox-church">Russian Orthodox Church</a>, created the bishopric of Perm in 1383 and consecrated Stephen as its first <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishop</a>. He settled at Ust-Vym and preached in Komi, and most of the Komi were baptised.</p><p>The new bishopric threatened Novgorod's control of the region's tribute. In 1385 Archbishop Aleksei of Novgorod sent an army to remove it, but the bishopric, helped by the city of Ustyug, defeated it. In 1386 Stephen visited Novgorod, and the city and its archbishop formally accepted the new situation. Afterwards the tribute went to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, part of a wider shift of the northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fur-trade">fur trade</a> from Novgorod to Moscow.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Stephen died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> on 26 April 1396 and was canonised by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-orthodox-church">Russian Orthodox Church</a> in 1549. He is called the Enlightener of Perm or the Apostle of the Permians, is regarded as the founder of the Permian written tradition, and is commemorated on 26 April. The historian Serge Aleksandrovich Zenkovsky wrote that he, Epiphanius the Wise, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sergius-of-radonezh">Sergius of Radonezh</a> and the painter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andrei-rublev">Andrei Rublev</a> signified the Russian spiritual and cultural revival of the late 14th and early 15th century. Epiphanius wrote the Life of Stephen of Perm and a Panegyric to him, styling him the creator of Permian letters.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Stephen of Perm” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In the late 14th century <b>Saint Stephen of Perm</b> converted many of the Komi and created the Old Permic alphabet for their language.</blockquote><a href="/state/great-perm">Great Perm</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/veliky-ustyug">Veliky Ustyug</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=60.76,46.3&amp;year=1376" aria-label="Veliky Ustyug on the atlas"><svg class="map map-globe map-loc" viewBox="0 0 10000 10000" role="img" aria-label="Globe: the birthplace of Stephen of Perm"><image href="/maps/globes/g060n60.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="10000"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4433 4876h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4433 4876h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=60.76,46.3&amp;year=1376">What was here in 1376? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
